{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BwRb4shgKdSY12wgSETRn1","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\paragraph{Model scale and architecture.}\n2\tOur primary experiments span 7--14B parameters: DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, and Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct (\\S\\ref{sec:scale-14b}). Whether the phase-modulated severity patterns persist, amplify, or attenuate at substantially larger scales (e.g., 70B) remains untested. Cross-architecture validation on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B (LlamaForCausalLM, \\S\\ref{sec:cross-arch}, Appendix~\\ref{app:cross-arch}) supports that the phase gradient extends beyond the Qwen2 architecture ($n{=}250$, all 8 conditions significant; importance $d_z{=}0.81$--$1.15$), but strategy specificity is substantially weaker ($2.0$--$2.6{\\times}$ vs.\\ $15$--$17{\\times}$ on DeepSeek) because random eviction also exhibits significant phase gradients on LLaMA ($d_z{=}0.41$--$0.46$). This architecture-dependent strategy specificity indicates that the degree of strategy${\\times}$phase interaction, not just absolute magnitude, varies across attention implementations. The pattern has been tested on two architecture families (Qwen2ForCausalLM, LlamaForCausalLM) at two scale points (7B, 14B); comprehensive validation across more diverse architectures (e.g., Mistral, multi-latent attention) and larger scales is needed for full generalization claims.\n3\t\n4\t\\paragraph{Task coverage.}\n5\tWe evaluate on three benchmarks spanning mathematical and scientific reasoning: MATH-500 (primary), GSM8K (\\S\\ref{sec:cross-benchmark}), and GPQA Diamond (\\S\\ref{sec:cross-benchmark-gpqa}). Cross-benchmark validation on GSM8K supports the phase gradient direction under importance-based eviction but reveals that random eviction is non-significant (CIs spanning zero), precluding meaningful ratio computation. On MATH-500, strategy specificity is quantifiable ($15$--$17{\\times}$); on GSM8K, it manifests as a qualitative gap (significant vs.\\ non-significant). This suggests that the phase-strategy interaction is robust in direction but task-dependent in form: complex reasoning (MATH-500) produces quantitative strategy contrast while simpler arithmetic (GSM8K) produces qualitative separation. Qwen GSM8K outputs were truncated at \\texttt{max\\_new\\_tokens}${=}$2047, so Qwen GSM8K absolute values carry this limitation; DeepSeek GSM8K data is untruncated. Our task-complexity explanation for specificity attenuation (lower cognitive load $\\to$ more uniform attention $\\to$ reduced strategy sensitivity) is supported by within-benchmark correlational evidence but requires validation across a broader range of task complexities; whether different reasoning tasks (code generation, multi-hop QA) produce yet different attenuation profiles remains open. Random eviction on GSM8K exhibits numerical instability (extreme outlier II values), likely a systematic interaction between reduced-precision arithmetic and score-independent eviction.\n6\t\n7\t\\paragraph{Eviction strategy scope and H$_2$O confound.}\n8\tOur importance-based eviction uses cumulative attention scores (H$_2$O-style scoring~\\cite{h2o-neurips2023}, Eq.~\\ref{eq:kv-importance}), which creates an inherent confound: head pruning alters the attention distribution from which importance scores are computed, and eviction then operates on these degraded scores. The compound interaction patterns we report, including importance discrimination collapse and the phase-strategy crossover, may be partly attributable to this circular dependency rather than to compound sparsity \\emph{per se}. Other attention-mass-based methods (SnapKV~\\cite{snapkv-neurips2024}, PyramidKV~\\cite{pyramidkv-colm2025}) share the same scoring basis and would likely exhibit a similar confound. The recency control (\\S\\ref{sec:scoring-control}) partially addresses this by confirming that the phase gradient requires the attention-score pathway ($d_z{=}0.083$, NS under recency eviction vs.\\ $d_z{=}0.574$ under importance at matched $s{=}0.15$). However, recency eviction was tested at a single sparsity level ($n{=}100$ pilot, vs.\\ $n{=}500$ for importance); comprehensive evaluation across sparsity levels, and testing of other score-independent strategies (e.g., learned eviction policies), remains an important next step. The qualitative conclusion (gradient absence, CI spanning zero) is robust to sample size: post-hoc power analysis indicates $n{=}100$ provides power $> 0.85$ to detect effects of $d_z \\geq 0.30$ at $\\alpha{=}0.05$, well below the observed importance effect ($d_z{=}0.57$).\n9\t\n10\t\\paragraph{Symmetric sparsity assumption.}\n11\tOur analysis applies symmetric sparsity levels: the same fraction $s$ is used for both head pruning and KV cache eviction. A preliminary spot check under asymmetric allocation (Appendix~\\ref{app:asymmetric-robustness}) supports that the phase gradient is robust to head/KV budget ratios, but systematic characterization of the full asymmetric interaction surface remains future work.\n12\t\n13\t\\paragraph{Phase segmentation validation.}\n14\tThe \\texttt{<think>} token validation of sparsity-based phase segmentation (\\S\\ref{sec:background}, Appendix~\\ref{app:think-validation}) is limited to a single model (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B) on a single benchmark (GSM8K, $n{=}48$). Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct does not generate explicit reasoning markers, precluding the same validation. Whether the high agreement ($\\kappa{=}0.88$) generalizes to other models with explicit CoT delimiters, or to non-mathematical reasoning tasks, remains open.\n15\t\n16\t\\paragraph{Mechanism.}\n17\tImportance discrimination collapse is a correlational diagnostic observation, not a causal explanation: both strategies exhibit near-identical Gini drops yet severity diverges by $15$--$17{\\times}$, indicating that Gini collapse alone is insufficient. Causal validation through controlled entropy manipulation is needed. The entropy redistribution component replicates on only 2/4 Qwen conditions, suggesting model-specific factors.\n18\t\n19\t\\paragraph{Perplexity vs.\\ accuracy.}\n20\tOur primary severity metric is perplexity-based (II), chosen because its per-token granularity enables the phase-level decomposition central to our analysis; accuracy is binary per-problem and cannot be meaningfully decomposed by reasoning phase. Accuracy validation on MATH-500 (\\S\\ref{sec:accuracy-validation}, Appendix~\\ref{app:accuracy-details}) shows gradual decline through $s{=}0.25$ ($\\delta{=}-4$ to $-13$\\,pp) followed by a sharp cliff at $s{=}0.30$ ($\\delta{=}-27$\\,pp) for Qwen ($n{=}100$, truncation $\\leq 4\\%$). DeepSeek accuracy deltas are confounded by rising truncation ($16\\%$ to $76\\%$); non-truncated analysis reveals a dual-regime structure but is limited by small surviving samples at high sparsity. Comprehensive accuracy evaluation across benchmarks remains an important next step.\n21\t\n22\t\\paragraph{Sparsity range.}\n23\tOur experiments span $s \\in \\{0.15, 0.20, 0.25, 0.30\\}$, sufficient to establish monotonicity at the tested levels (JT test $p \\ll 10^{-10}$) but insufficient to characterize the functional form of the auxiliary ratio $G(s)$ or identify potential saturation thresholds. More extreme sparsity ($s > 0.50$) may exhibit qualitatively different interaction dynamics. We do not report inference throughput or latency measurements, which are necessary for a complete deployment analysis but orthogonal to our severity characterization. Extended limitations in Appendix~\\ref{app:limitations}.\n24\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01HKVWsaRz8eKfso824zLA5e","session_id":"4a5aac10-195d-47e9-909e-6fb532527f79","uuid":"f6bf29f0-76ed-4ff2-819e-232dd8a72880","timestamp":"2026-05-17T01:25:45.165Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Wiy8K8hNDqmmiz7X1rbLnX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01NutjZeWbamL8GUt6agkJ6S","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an academic paper review at a top ML venue (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\nYour focus areas: **Novelty/Significance** and **Related Work positioning**.\n\n## Paper: \"Phase-Gated Compound Sparsity: Reasoning Phase Modulates the Severity of Joint Attention Pruning and KV Cache Eviction\"\n\n### Summary\nThe paper studies \"compound sparsity\" (joint attention head pruning + KV cache eviction) and claims that the resulting degradation is not uniform but varies systematically across reasoning phases. Using DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B on MATH-500, GSM8K, and GPQA Diamond, they measure a per-problem \"interaction index\" across 4 sparsity levels and 3 reasoning phases. \n\n### Key claims/contributions:\n1. Discovery that compound sparsity severity is phase-dependent (importance-based eviction: dz 0.61→1.13 across sparsity levels)\n2. Novel methodological control: recency-based eviction as scoring-independent control to confirm attention-score mechanism\n3. Cross-benchmark validation (MATH-500, GSM8K, GPQA Diamond): phase gradient universal, strategy specificity task-modulated (15×→2.5×)\n4. Boundary condition at extreme sparsity where any eviction produces weak phase effects\n\n### Related Work Coverage (§2)\nThe paper covers 5 categories:\n- **Compound Compression**: SparK (uniform super-additive), Ada-KV, SLiM\n- **Phase-Aware KV Compression**: ThinKV (phase-aware for CoT), R-KV (reasoning-specific)\n- **Compression Phase Transitions**: Physics of KV cache compression (compression-rate regimes vs reasoning phases)\n- **Importance Score Collapse**: HIES (entropy-based compensation for score collapse)\n- **Adaptive Eviction Methods**: Lethe (layer-time-adaptive pruning)\n\nKey positioning claims:\n- vs ThinKV/R-KV: they do single-axis KV compression, this paper does compound (dual-axis) interaction\n- vs SparK: treats compound interaction as uniform, this paper shows it's phase-modulated\n- vs HIES: single-axis score collapse, this paper shows compound amplification\n- vs Physics-of-KV: \"phase\" means compression-rate regime, not reasoning phase\n\n### What the paper does NOT do:\n- Does not propose a new compression method\n- Does not optimize inference speed\n- Is purely empirical/analytical\n- Limited to 7B models, 2 architectures\n\n### Paper Structure\n7 pages: Abstract, Introduction (1.5p), Related Work (1p), Method (1p), Experiments (2p), Discussion (1p), Conclusion (0.5p). No figures at all — only 6 tables. No appendix.\n\n### Your Review Task\n\nProvide a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment**: \n   - Is the core finding (phase-dependent compound severity) genuinely novel? Or is it a relatively expected consequence of known phenomena (score collapse under pruning, phase-dependent reasoning)?\n   - How significant is the contribution given it's an empirical finding paper with no proposed method?\n   - Is the recency-based control genuinely novel as a methodological contribution?\n   - Does the \"boundary condition\" finding add meaningful insight or is it obvious?\n\n2. **Related Work Quality**:\n   - Is the coverage sufficient? Are there missing related works?\n   - Is the positioning fair and accurate?\n   - Does the paper clearly establish the gap it fills?\n   - Are there other works studying compound compression interactions that should be cited?\n\n3. **Significance & Impact**:\n   - Who benefits from this finding?\n   - Does it change how practitioners should think about compound compression?\n   - Is the contribution incremental over existing phase-aware methods like ThinKV?\n   - Without a proposed method or practical guidelines, is the empirical finding alone sufficient for a top venue?\n\n4. **Framing & Claims**:\n   - Are the claims appropriately scoped?\n   - Is \"phase-gated\" the right framing, or is this better described as \"perplexity-dependent degradation\"?\n   - The paper claims 4 contributions — are all genuinely distinct contributions?\n\n5. **Presentation Assessment**:\n   - No figures at all — is this a problem for a paper about gradients and dose-response patterns?\n   - Is the writing quality appropriate?\n   - Is the paper self-contained?\n\nOutput your review in this format:\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty & Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Weaknesses  \n[Numbered list with detailed explanations]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Novelty Assessment\n[Detailed paragraph]\n\n### Related Work Gaps\n[What's missing]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Presentation, writing, etc.]\n\n### Scores\n- Novelty: X/4\n- Significance: X/4\n- Related Work: X/4\n- Overall Score: X/6\n- Confidence: X/5\n```\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":24415,"cache_read_input_tokens":24215,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":24415},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4a5aac10-195d-47e9-909e-6fb532527f79","uuid":"dcfc932f-75ee-4580-a8ae-89a41472de9b"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a02256d22d8b92a6a","tool_use_id":"toolu_01NutjZeWbamL8GUt6agkJ6S","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an academic paper review at a top ML venue (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\nYour focus areas: **Novelty/Significance** and **Related Work positioning**.\n\n## Paper: \"Phase-Gated Compound Sparsity: Reasoning Phase Modulates the Severity of Joint Attention Pruning and KV Cache Eviction\"\n\n### Summary\nThe paper studies \"compound sparsity\" (joint attention head pruning + KV cache eviction) and claims that the resulting degradation is not uniform but varies systematically across reasoning phases. Using DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B on MATH-500, GSM8K, and GPQA Diamond, they measure a per-problem \"interaction index\" across 4 sparsity levels and 3 reasoning phases. \n\n### Key claims/contributions:\n1. Discovery that compound sparsity severity is phase-dependent (importance-based eviction: dz 0.61→1.13 across sparsity levels)\n2. Novel methodological control: recency-based eviction as scoring-independent control to confirm attention-score mechanism\n3. Cross-benchmark validation (MATH-500, GSM8K, GPQA Diamond): phase gradient universal, strategy specificity task-modulated (15×→2.5×)\n4. Boundary condition at extreme sparsity where any eviction produces weak phase effects\n\n### Related Work Coverage (§2)\nThe paper covers 5 categories:\n- **Compound Compression**: SparK (uniform super-additive), Ada-KV, SLiM\n- **Phase-Aware KV Compression**: ThinKV (phase-aware for CoT), R-KV (reasoning-specific)\n- **Compression Phase Transitions**: Physics of KV cache compression (compression-rate regimes vs reasoning phases)\n- **Importance Score Collapse**: HIES (entropy-based compensation for score collapse)\n- **Adaptive Eviction Methods**: Lethe (layer-time-adaptive pruning)\n\nKey positioning claims:\n- vs ThinKV/R-KV: they do single-axis KV compression, this paper does compound (dual-axis) interaction\n- vs SparK: treats compound interaction as uniform, this paper shows it's phase-modulated\n- vs HIES: single-axis score collapse, this paper shows compound amplification\n- vs Physics-of-KV: \"phase\" means compression-rate regime, not reasoning phase\n\n### What the paper does NOT do:\n- Does not propose a new compression method\n- Does not optimize inference speed\n- Is purely empirical/analytical\n- Limited to 7B models, 2 architectures\n\n### Paper Structure\n7 pages: Abstract, Introduction (1.5p), Related Work (1p), Method (1p), Experiments (2p), Discussion (1p), Conclusion (0.5p). No figures at all — only 6 tables. No appendix.\n\n### Your Review Task\n\nProvide a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment**: \n   - Is the core finding (phase-dependent compound severity) genuinely novel? Or is it a relatively expected consequence of known phenomena (score collapse under pruning, phase-dependent reasoning)?\n   - How significant is the contribution given it's an empirical finding paper with no proposed method?\n   - Is the recency-based control genuinely novel as a methodological contribution?\n   - Does the \"boundary condition\" finding add meaningful insight or is it obvious?\n\n2. **Related Work Quality**:\n   - Is the coverage sufficient? Are there missing related works?\n   - Is the positioning fair and accurate?\n   - Does the paper clearly establish the gap it fills?\n   - Are there other works studying compound compression interactions that should be cited?\n\n3. **Significance & Impact**:\n   - Who benefits from this finding?\n   - Does it change how practitioners should think about compound compression?\n   - Is the contribution incremental over existing phase-aware methods like ThinKV?\n   - Without a proposed method or practical guidelines, is the empirical finding alone sufficient for a top venue?\n\n4. **Framing & Claims**:\n   - Are the claims appropriately scoped?\n   - Is \"phase-gated\" the right framing, or is this better described as \"perplexity-dependent degradation\"?\n   - The paper claims 4 contributions — are all genuinely distinct contributions?\n\n5. **Presentation Assessment**:\n   - No figures at all — is this a problem for a paper about gradients and dose-response patterns?\n   - Is the writing quality appropriate?\n   - Is the paper self-contained?\n\nOutput your review in this format:\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty & Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Weaknesses  \n[Numbered list with detailed explanations]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Novelty Assessment\n[Detailed paragraph]\n\n### Related Work Gaps\n[What's missing]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Presentation, writing, etc.]\n\n### Scores\n- Novelty: X/4\n- Significance: X/4\n- Related Work: X/4\n- Overall Score: X/6\n- Confidence: X/5\n```\n","uuid":"cc3b58c7-9189-4fd7-b351-25857b7d2e34","session_id":"4a5aac10-195d-47e9-909e-6fb532527f79"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an academic paper review at a top ML venue (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\nYour focus areas: **Novelty/Significance** and **Related Work positioning**.\n\n## Paper: \"Phase-Gated Compound Sparsity: Reasoning Phase Modulates the Severity of Joint Attention Pruning and KV Cache Eviction\"\n\n### Summary\nThe paper studies \"compound sparsity\" (joint attention head pruning + KV cache eviction) and claims that the resulting degradation is not uniform but varies systematically across reasoning phases. Using DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B on MATH-500, GSM8K, and GPQA Diamond, they measure a per-problem \"interaction index\" across 4 sparsity levels and 3 reasoning phases. \n\n### Key claims/contributions:\n1. Discovery that compound sparsity severity is phase-dependent (importance-based eviction: dz 0.61→1.13 across sparsity levels)\n2. Novel methodological control: recency-based eviction as scoring-independent control to confirm attention-score mechanism\n3. Cross-benchmark validation (MATH-500, GSM8K, GPQA Diamond): phase gradient universal, strategy specificity task-modulated (15×→2.5×)\n4. Boundary condition at extreme sparsity where any eviction produces weak phase effects\n\n### Related Work Coverage (§2)\nThe paper covers 5 categories:\n- **Compound Compression**: SparK (uniform super-additive), Ada-KV, SLiM\n- **Phase-Aware KV Compression**: ThinKV (phase-aware for CoT), R-KV (reasoning-specific)\n- **Compression Phase Transitions**: Physics of KV cache compression (compression-rate regimes vs reasoning phases)\n- **Importance Score Collapse**: HIES (entropy-based compensation for score collapse)\n- **Adaptive Eviction Methods**: Lethe (layer-time-adaptive pruning)\n\nKey positioning claims:\n- vs ThinKV/R-KV: they do single-axis KV compression, this paper does compound (dual-axis) interaction\n- vs SparK: treats compound interaction as uniform, this paper shows it's phase-modulated\n- vs HIES: single-axis score collapse, this paper shows compound amplification\n- vs Physics-of-KV: \"phase\" means compression-rate regime, not reasoning phase\n\n### What the paper does NOT do:\n- Does not propose a new compression method\n- Does not optimize inference speed\n- Is purely empirical/analytical\n- Limited to 7B models, 2 architectures\n\n### Paper Structure\n7 pages: Abstract, Introduction (1.5p), Related Work (1p), Method (1p), Experiments (2p), Discussion (1p), Conclusion (0.5p). No figures at all — only 6 tables. No appendix.\n\n### Your Review Task\n\nProvide a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment**: \n   - Is the core finding (phase-dependent compound severity) genuinely novel? Or is it a relatively expected consequence of known phenomena (score collapse under pruning, phase-dependent reasoning)?\n   - How significant is the contribution given it's an empirical finding paper with no proposed method?\n   - Is the recency-based control genuinely novel as a methodological contribution?\n   - Does the \"boundary condition\" finding add meaningful insight or is it obvious?\n\n2. **Related Work Quality**:\n   - Is the coverage sufficient? Are there missing related works?\n   - Is the positioning fair and accurate?\n   - Does the paper clearly establish the gap it fills?\n   - Are there other works studying compound compression interactions that should be cited?\n\n3. **Significance & Impact**:\n   - Who benefits from this finding?\n   - Does it change how practitioners should think about compound compression?\n   - Is the contribution incremental over existing phase-aware methods like ThinKV?\n   - Without a proposed method or practical guidelines, is the empirical finding alone sufficient for a top venue?\n\n4. **Framing & Claims**:\n   - Are the claims appropriately scoped?\n   - Is \"phase-gated\" the right framing, or is this better described as \"perplexity-dependent degradation\"?\n   - The paper claims 4 contributions — are all genuinely distinct contributions?\n\n5. **Presentation Assessment**:\n   - No figures at all — is this a problem for a paper about gradients and dose-response patterns?\n   - Is the writing quality appropriate?\n   - Is the paper self-contained?\n\nOutput your review in this format:\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty & Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Weaknesses  \n[Numbered list with detailed explanations]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Novelty Assessment\n[Detailed paragraph]\n\n### Related Work Gaps\n[What's missing]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Presentation, writing, etc.]\n\n### Scores\n- Novelty: X/4\n- Significance: X/4\n- Related Work: X/4\n- Overall Score: X/6\n- Confidence: X/5\n```\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01NutjZeWbamL8GUt6agkJ6S","session_id":"4a5aac10-195d-47e9-909e-6fb532527f79","uuid":"5ce6543b-2fa2-484a-8435-9beec9f4ec66","timestamp":"2026-05-17T01:25:46.622Z"}
